  {
   "title": "No verb needed",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "t": "Unlike English, a Tagalog sentence with an indirect object **does not need a verb**. Often the action is only implied, as in these sentences with the demonstrative pronouns **itó** (this) and **iyán** (that)."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "t": "Each pair below shows two word orders. In either order the meaning stays the same, and there is still no verb."
    },
    {
     "type": "examples",
     "items": [
      {
       "tl": "Itó ay regalo ko para sa iyó.",
       "en": "This is my gift for you.",
